    <summary>Open-source calendar for Windows with repeating appointments, reminders, color categories, four views, twenty languages and optional encryption.</summary>
    <description>Free and open-source calendar for Windows with no account, no sync and no telemetry. Your appointments live in a single .kcal file on your own machine, an ordinary SQLite database until you put a password on it. Install or run portable.

- Four views, month, week, day and agenda, with one shared previous / next / today control; month zooms from one to six visible weeks and draws multi-day appointments as continuous runs
- Appointments edited in a slide-out sidebar panel rather than a dialog: title, start and end, all-day, location, description and attendees
- Repeating appointments, daily, weekly on the days you tick, monthly on a date or on the first to fourth or last weekday, or yearly, every N of those, ending never, after a count or on a date, with per-date exceptions; editing or deleting one date offers that date, this and following, or the whole series
- Reminders with snooze and dismiss, shown when they come due and caught up after Killendar was closed
- Search, undo, a Recently deleted list kept for 30 days, and verified daily backups that restore as a new Killendar
- Duplicate an appointment, save templates, set your work days and hours, see when appointments overlap, and move them by keyboard
- Optional time zones per appointment, with a second zone's hours beside your own in Week and Day
- Drag to move appointments across times and days; overlapping appointments sit side by side instead of hiding one behind the other
- Color categories you define, painting every view, plus a grid density control down to quarter hours
- Import and export in the formats people actually hand you: iCalendar (.ics, RFC 5545) both ways, CSV in Outlook's column format both ways, saved email invites (.eml) in, and a themed web page out in Month, Week, Day or Agenda layout with a print stylesheet; double-clicking an .ics file imports it
- Optional encryption: the lock button puts a password on a Killendar and encrypts it at rest with SQLCipher (AES-256, per-page HMAC-SHA512); opt-in, so no password means a plain readable SQLite file
- As many Killendars as you like, work, on-call, family, each its own .kcal file with its own password or none, kept in the default profile folder, another folder you choose, or beside the portable executable; show others beside the open one, each in its own color and read only
- An agenda command line, and KillerMCP can read the agenda and create appointments
- Thirteen themes including a full 98SE recreation; Dark, Light, Black and 98SE each carry eight accent colors for 41 looks in all
- Localized in twenty languages, switchable without a restart
- Keyboard driven with single-key shortcuts and a full shortcut overlay on F1, with list and visual keyboard views
- Verified code signing shown in the About card, checked with WinVerifyTrust rather than read off the file
- One self-contained Windows EXE of about 6.5 MB, no runtime install; runs portable, self-installs per-user without UAC or machine-wide with /silent for scripted deployment, and uninstalls without deleting your appointments
- GPLv3, local-only, no account, no telemetry. The only connection it makes on its own is a check for updates at startup, which can be turned off in About
    </description>
    <releaseNotes>https://github.com/SteveTheKiller/Killendar/releases/tag/vREPLACE_VERSION</releaseNotes>
